[{:seon.task/name "a-time-predicate-read-keeps-index-order"
  :seon.task/plan "Done (Datahike e5a55664, jolt/time-pred-order). Found by worktree session ctx-atom (2026-10-26) while building pull's :reverse option (Datahike 872bfa03, docs/forks.md#datahike). A db read through a time predicate (as-of, since) answers its datoms out of index order once a read spans more than eight [e a] groups, and its rseek-datoms realizes the index from the seek point down to the index's start. Reproduction, on a :keep-history? store: an entity root and ten children, each written in its own transaction with [:parent root]; as of the sixth child's transaction, (d/datoms asof :avet :parent root) answers six datoms, and (take 6 (d/rseek-datoms asof :avet :parent root)) answers [7 9 9 \"c5\" 6 …], out of order and leaving the [:parent root] prefix (with four children both answer in order). Cause: datahike.db/post-process-datoms, for a context with a time predicate and not historical, transduces the datoms into a map grouped by [e a] (group-by-step) and assembles each group's current datoms (assemble-datoms-xform): Clojure's array map keeps insertion order up to eight entries and a hash map after, so the answer's order is the hash's; the transduce is eager, so over rseek-datoms' open range (down to the index's start) it reads everything below the seek point. Anything reading an as-of or since db in index order (a pull's collection, a seek, a newest-n window, a merge of two sorted reads) can be wrong. Correct, in Datahike on a jolt/<topic> branch: a time-predicate read keeps index order (each [e a] group's current datoms emitted where the group starts, in the order the groups were read) and stays lazy, so a seek or rseek reads what it is taken for. Pull's :reverse sorts the bounded prefix on such a db (datahike.pull-api/reverse-datoms), so its :limit n there reads the whole collection; this task deletes that sort branch and its datahike.datom require, leaving rseek-datoms the one read. Proof: datahike.test.time-predicate-order-test/a-time-predicate-read-keeps-index-order (ten children as of the sixth: datoms and rseek-datoms in index order, rseek ends at the prefix), and Datahike's own suite answering the same before and after."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{datahike.test.time-predicate-order-test/a-time-predicate-read-keeps-index-order}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-diff-records-its-reads"
  :seon.task/plan "Found by root's render-not-eval experiment, 2026-10-27 (blocker 1): d/datoms-diff (reference-code/datahike/src/datahike/db.cljc, datoms-diff) reaches psi/diff past the index protocol (datahike.index.interface), whose -slice, -rslice, -lookup and -count-slice record each range under d/with-reads (di/record-read!), so a read built on a diff recorded nothing: a render over a diff (merge readiness as a render) was skipped after its branch added a program key, keeping its old answer. Fix in the fork (jolt/a-diff-records-its-reads, 65dad766): psi/diff records its [from to] range as -slice does, so the read's key is the diffed attribute ([:a a]) or :any, and seon.render's walk matches a transaction against it like any read (render!, frame!). Coarse by design: any datom in the diffed range touches it, as a slice's does. Replaces nothing; the alternative, a render passing the diff's inputs as its children, is a second way to say what it read. Seen on the way: datahike.test.datoms-diff-test/datoms-diff-equals-the-plain-set-difference-over-generated-histories fails on Jolt with and without this change (seed 20261015, a counterexample of 11 operations), a diff that differs from the plain set difference: a defect of its own, not yet filed as a task."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{datahike.test.datoms-diff-test/a-diff-records-the-range-it-read seon.render-test/a-render-over-a-diff-runs-again-when-the-diff-moves}}]
